  "reasoning": "The patient presents with headaches, chest pains, fainting episodes, and difficulty focusing. These symptoms strongly align with hypertension. Training reference [1] describes a nearly identical clinical picture ('headaches, chest pains, and dizziness... losing my balance and I can't focus') and correctly diagnoses hypertension. Fainting is a severe manifestation of the dizziness and cerebral hypoperfusion often associated with uncontrolled high blood pressure, while difficulty focusing reflects the cognitive impact of hypertensive encephalopathy or vascular strain. Other listed conditions are ruled out due to the absence of their hallmark symptoms: no rashes or skin changes (rules out psoriasis, impetigo, fungal infection, drug reaction), no respiratory distress or cough (rules out bronchial asthma, pneumonia, common cold), no urinary symptoms (rules out UTI), no gastrointestinal complaints (rules out GERD, PUD), no joint pain or stiffness (rules out arthritis, cervical spondylosis), and no fever or bleeding tendencies (rules out malaria, dengue, typhoid). Diabetes would present with polyuria/polydipsia, and allergies typically involve sneezing, rhinorrhea, or throat swelling. Thus, hypertension is the only diagnosis supported by the full symptom cluster.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]hypertension[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
